The  file  contains a list of mail addresses or programs that the
user’s mail should be redirected to.  If the file is not present,
then no mail forwarding will be done.  Mail may also be forwarded
as the standard input to a program by prefixing the line with the
normal shell pipe symbol (|).  If arguments are to be  passed  to
the  command,  then the entire line should be enclosed in quotes.
For security reasons, the file must be owned by the user the mail
is being sent to, or by root, and the user’s shell must be listed
in For example, if a file  contained  the  following  lines:  no‐
body@NetBSD.org  "|/usr/bin/vacation  nobody"  Mail would be for‐
warded to and to the program with the single argument If a  local
user  address is prefixed with a backslash character, mail is de‐
livered directly to the user’s mail spool file, bypassing further
redirection.  For example, if user chris had  a  file  containing
the  following  lines:  chris@otherhost  \chris  One copy of mail
would be forwarded to and another copy would be retained as  mail
for local user chris.  The user’s forwarding instructions.
